      "worked_solution": "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,g(x)\\le y\\le f(x)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type I region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $x:$\n\n$$\nx=y+1\\,⟹\\,y=x-1\n$$\n\nThe function $y=\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}$ is already written as a function of $x.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the vertical lines $x=1$ and $x=3.$ So, we have $1\\le x\\le 3.$\n\nWe draw a vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower function $g(x)$ and the upper function $f(x).$ A vertical line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type I region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14096470/assets/15f9c5d526747b80766a8a3250fa2e705d5d7d9561c52e08492b7a18f1483eb6.png)\n\nThe vertical line enters the region through the lower function $y=\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}$ and leaves through the upper function $y=x-1.$ So, we have $\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}\\le y\\le x-1.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}\\le y\\le x-1\\}.\n$$",
